The fountain located in Piazza del Campo in Siena is the **Fonte Gaia** ("Fountain of Joy"). 

First inaugurated in 1346 to celebrate the arrival of water into the public square via an extensive network of underground aqueducts (*bottini*), it received its famous sculpted marble basins and relief panels between 1409 and 1419, crafted by Sienese sculptor Jacopo della Quercia. The fountain currently standing in the square is a 19th-century replica created by Tito Sarrocchi (1869) to protect the weathered original panels, which are preserved in the Santa Maria della Scala museum complex across from the Siena Cathedral.

